UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 20, Lesson 1: Performing and evaluating dance', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Evaluating dance uses criteria such as clear motif, varied levels, controlled energy, musical timing and expressive intent. Give evidence from what you saw, not from friendship. Kind feedback names two strengths and one workable wish.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nWrite feedback for a dance that had a clear theme but rushed transitions.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Stars: theme readable; good pathway variety. Wish: slow counts 7–8 to tidy the transition.''\n\nWATCH FOR\nOnly saying ''good'' or commenting on looks. Criteria-based evidence is required.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nPerform your themed dance and complete a self-evaluation against five criteria plus peer two stars and a wish.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Creative Arts","learning_intention":"Evaluating dance uses criteria such as clear motif, varied levels, controlled energy, musical timing and expressive intent. Give evidence from what you saw, not from friendship. Kind feedback names two strengths and one workable wish.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Explore and learn","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["CA2-DAN-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Perform your themed dance and complete a self-evaluation against five criteria plus peer two stars and a wish. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Strong answer: ''Stars: theme readable; good pathway variety. Wish: slow counts 7–8 to tidy the transition.'' Reasoning: I used evaluate by attending to criteria rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Only saying ''good'' or commenting on looks. Criteria-based evidence is required. Correction: Strong answer: ''Stars: theme readable; good pathway variety. Wish: slow counts 7–8 to tidy the transition.'' Why: the correction restores accurate use of evaluate.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Only saying ''good'' or commenting on looks. Criteria-based evidence is required. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating evaluate on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with evaluate, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Strong answer: ''Stars: theme readable; good pathway variety. Wish: slow counts 7–8 to tidy the transition.'' This matches the lesson skill for evaluate.","options":["Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work.","Strong answer: ''Stars: theme readable; good pathway variety. Wish: slow counts 7–8 to tidy the transition.''","Only saying ''good'' or commenting on looks. Criteria-based evidence is required."],"question":"Write feedback for a dance that had a clear theme but rushed transitions. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Only saying ''good'' or commenting on looks. Criteria-based evidence is required. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Careful use of criteria with clear evidence.","Only saying ''good'' or commenting on looks. Criteria-based evidence is required.","Evaluating dance uses criteria such as clear motif, varied levels, controlled energy, musical timing and expressive intent. Give evidence from what you saw,…","Strong answer: ''Stars: theme readable; good pathway variety. Wish: slow counts 7–8 to tidy the transition.''"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising evaluate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named criteria, not by copying the model.","options":["Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Invent a sacred First Nations design without permission or guidance.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the criteria used."],"question":"A learner is practising evaluate. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Strong answer: ''Stars: theme readable; good pathway variety. Wish: slow counts 7–8 to tidy the transition.'' uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work.","Strong answer: ''Stars: theme readable; good pathway variety. Wish: slow counts 7–8 to tidy the transition.''","Only saying ''good'' or commenting on looks. Criteria-based evidence is required.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named."],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about evaluate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue.","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail.","Only saying ''good'' or commenting on looks. Criteria-based evidence is required.","Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about evaluate."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about criteria?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Example: ''Star: motif repeated in ABA. Star: strong/light contrast. Wish: land the jump on count 8 with softer knees.''","Only saying ''good'' or commenting on looks. Criteria-based evidence is required.","Invent a sacred First Nations design without permission or guidance.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Evaluating dance uses criteria such as clear motif, varied levels, controlled energy, musical timing and… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete.","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every online claim as equally reliable."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Only saying ''good'' or commenting on looks. Criteria-based evidence is required.","Perform your themed dance and complete a self-evaluation against five criteria plus peer two stars and a wish.","Retype the worked example with no new example.","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work."],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of evaluate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating evaluate on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Strong answer: ''Stars: theme readable; good pathway variety. Wish: slow counts 7–8 to tidy the transition.'' Reasoning: I used evaluate by attending to criteria rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with evaluate, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Only saying ''good'' or commenting on looks. Criteria-based evidence is required. Correction: Strong answer: ''Stars: theme readable; good pathway variety. Wish: slow counts 7–8 to tidy the transition.'' Why: the correction restores accurate use of evaluate.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-arts-y4-w20-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Creative Arts alignment using catalogue outcome codes (CA2-*); content-point mapping remains pending. First Nations arts content requires local Aboriginal educator or Custodian guidance before acceptance; do not invent sacred designs.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise evaluate.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Dance","success_criteria":["I can use evaluate with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify criteria and explain why it matters.","I can practise evidence independently on a new prompt.","I can notice kind feedback needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Evaluating dance uses criteria such as clear motif, varied levels, controlled energy, musical timing and expressive intent. Give evidence from what you saw, not from friendship. Kind feedback names two strengths and one workable wish. Supported responses name the element or…","options":["Evaluating dance uses criteria such as clear motif, varied levels, controlled energy, musical timing and expressive intent. Give evidence from what you saw, not from friendship. Kind feedback names two strengths and one workable wish.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about evaluate?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: ''Star: motif repeated in ABA. Star: strong/light contrast. Wish: land the jump on count 8 with softer knees.''\n\nTRY FIRST\nWrite feedback for a dance that had a clear theme but rushed transitions.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Stars: theme readable; good pathway variety. Wish: slow counts 7–8 to tidy the transition.''","explain":"Evaluating dance uses criteria such as clear motif, varied levels, controlled energy, musical timing and expressive intent. Give evidence from what you saw, not from friendship. Kind feedback names two strengths and one workable wish.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Only saying ''good'' or commenting on looks. Criteria-based evidence is required. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Evaluating dance.","caption":"Specific and kind.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Criteria</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">motif levels timing</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Evidence</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">what you saw</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: ''Star: motif repeated in ABA. Star: strong/light contrast. Wish: land the jump on count 8 with softer knees.'' Then write your own artistic reason.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.\nJuno: Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: ''Star: motif repeated in ABA. Star: strong/light contrast. Wish: land the jump on count 8 with softer knees.'' Then write your own artistic reason."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about evaluate?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: ''Star: motif repeated in ABA. Star: strong/light contrast. Wish: land the jump on count 8 with softer knees.'' Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"Feedback pattern.","caption":"Workable next step.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Stars</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">two strengths</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Wish</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">one fix</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["A guess with no artistic reason.","Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about evaluate?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nOnly saying ''good'' or commenting on looks. Criteria-based evidence is required. Here is a more helpful example: Example: ''Star: motif repeated in ABA. Star: strong/light contrast. Wish: land the jump on count 8 with softer knees.''\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice weakens the work, then write a stronger choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Stars: theme readable; good pathway variety. Wish: slow counts 7–8 to tidy the transition.''","explain":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the decoration without changing the artistic idea does not fix the error. Only saying ''good'' or commenting on looks. Criteria-based evidence is required. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about evaluate?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nEvaluating dance uses criteria such as clear motif, varied levels, controlled energy, musical timing and expressive intent. Give evidence from what you saw, not from friendship. Kind feedback names two strengths and one workable wish. Now choose a different subject or piece to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same arts idea as the model, but with different content or material.","explain":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new artwork or performance. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Transfer to a new artwork or performance"}],"title":"Year 4, Week 20, Lesson 1: Performing and evaluating dance","unit_number":4,"unit_title":"Space, time, energy and choreography","week":20,"week_in_unit":5,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: ''Star: motif repeated in ABA. Star: strong/light contrast. Wish: land the jump on count 8 with softer knees.''\n\nWrite what makes this example effective, naming the element, technique or performance choice and its effect.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Evaluating dance.","caption":"Specific and kind.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height